DOMAIN_OVERVIEW:
Habermas' critique of speculative philosophy of history is multidimensional.
Against Hegel, he targets the "philosophy of the subject" — the tendency to
subsume historical multiplicity under a self-realizing spirit — in The
Philosophical Discourse of Modernity. Against Marx, he argues that the
labour-paradigm cannot ground a theory of social evolution: the paradigm of
production must be displaced by the paradigm of communicative action, as
argued in "Toward a Reconstruction of Historical Materialism" and developed
in the Theory of Communicative Action. Against Adorno and Horkheimer, he
charges that the Dialectic of Enlightenment, by collapsing reason into
instrumental reason, forecloses the normative resources that critical theory
requires — leaving it with no standpoint from which to criticize modernity.

His Postmetaphysical Thinking (1992) generalizes these critiques into a
methodological stance: philosophy after Hegel must abandon the claim to be
the culminating discipline of all knowledge, surrendering to fallibilism,
proceduralism, and reconstructive method.

RELEVANCE_TO_PROJECT:
This domain establishes the negative dimension of Habermas' engagement with
philosophy of history: what he rejects and why. Without it, the constructive
alternative (Domains 3–5) cannot be understood. The critique of Hegel, Marx,
and the Frankfurt School is the condition of possibility for the reconstructive
project.

SYNTHESIS_GUIDANCE:
The key tension is between Habermas' desire to preserve the normative impetus
of the tradition and his rejection of its substantive-metaphysical pretensions.
He seeks a philosophy of history that retains universalist aspirations (moral
progress, the claim that some social formations are superior to others) while
abandoning the idea that history has a single subject or a determinate telos.
Synthesis should show how the three critiques — of Hegel, Marx, and Adorno —
converge on this dual movement.

KEY_POSITIONS:
- Habermas' primary critiques: 4 papers — philosophical discourse of modernity,
  postscript to KHI, postmetaphysical thinking, reconstruction of HM
- Secondary analysis of Habermas vs Hegel: 3 papers — Dallmayr, Cobben, Tang
- Habermas vs Frankfurt School: 3 papers — Hohendahl ×2, Freyenhagen
- Recent reassessments: 4 papers — Baynes, Verovšek, Czingon, Nascimento
====================================================================
}

@book{habermas1987philosophical,
  author = {Habermas, Jürgen},
  title = {The Philosophical Discourse of Modernity: Twelve Lectures},
  year = {1987},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Habermas traces the "discourse of modernity" from Hegel through
  Nietzsche, Heidegger, Derrida, Foucault, and the Young Conservatives. He argues
  that the tradition of the philosophy of the subject — inaugurated by Hegel's
  attempt to make Geist the carrier of a self-reflexive historical process —
  generates an antinomy: it can neither ground normative critique nor avoid
  collapsing reason into power. The solution is the "paradigm shift" from
  subject-centred to communicative reason, which abandons the metaphysical
  ambitions of speculative philosophy of history while retaining the normative
  content of the Enlightenment project.

  RELEVANCE: This is Habermas' most sustained and direct engagement with the
  tradition of speculative philosophy of history. It is the locus classicus of
  his critique of Hegel's "philosophy of the subject" and his argument that
  philosophy of history must be reconstructed rather than continued or abandoned.

  POSITION: Central primary source for the critique of speculative philosophy
  of history; foundational for understanding Habermas' relationship to Hegel,
  Nietzsche, Heidegger, and poststructuralism.
  },
  keywords = {philosophy-of-subject, modernity, Hegel, Nietzsche, postmodernism, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@book{horkheimerAdorno2002dialectic,
  author = {Horkheimer, Max and Adorno, Theodor W.},
  title = {Dialectic of Enlightenment},
  year = {2002},
  doi = {10.1515/9780804788090},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Horkheimer and Adorno argue that the Enlightenment's drive
  towards domination of nature through instrumental reason leads to the
  self-destruction of reason. Myth and Enlightenment are intertwined: the
  rationalisation of the world produces new forms of barbarism. History is
  not progress towards emancipation but a catastrophic dialectic in which
  rational mastery turns against human beings.

  RELEVANCE: This is the text against which Habermas defines his own position
  most explicitly. He inherits the Frankfurt School's concern with the pathologies
  of modernity but rejects the "total critique of reason" as performatively
  self-contradictory. His reconstruction of communicative rationality is partly
  a response to what he sees as the impasse Adorno and Horkheimer reach by
  equating all rationalization with instrumental reason.

  POSITION: Essential background; the negative foil against which Habermas
  articulates the communicative alternative in his critique of speculative
  philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {Frankfurt-School, instrumental-reason, enlightenment, modernity, High},
  abstract = {Dialectic of Enlightenment is, quite justifiably, one of the most celebrated and often cited works of modern social philosophy. It has been identified as the keystone of the 'Frankfurt School', of which Theodor Adorno and Max Horkheimer were the leading members, and does not cease to impress in its wide-ranging ambition and panache. Adorno and Horkheimer addressees themselves to a question which was felt with particular urgency in the aftermath of National Socialism: how was it possible that science, technology and rational thought could have led to barbarism rather than emancipation?},
  abstract_source = {s2},
}

@article{hohendahl1985dialectic,
  author = {Hohendahl, Peter U.},
  title = {The Dialectic of Enlightenment Revisited: Habermas' Critique of the Frankfurt School},
  journal = {New German Critique},
  year = {1985},
  number = {35},
  pages = {3},
  doi = {10.2307/488198},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Hohendahl examines Habermas' critique of Adorno and Horkheimer
  in detail, focusing on the charge that the Dialectic of Enlightenment forfeits
  any normative standard for critique by totalizing the concept of instrumental
  reason. He assesses whether Habermas' communicative rationality can do the
  critical work that Adorno's negative dialectics cannot, and identifies
  residual tensions in Habermas' account of the transition from first- to
  second-generation Frankfurt School.

  RELEVANCE: This is one of the earliest and most thorough secondary analyses
  of Habermas' critique of Adorno and Horkheimer. It maps the conceptual
  architecture of the disagreement and identifies the key stakes for any
  philosophy of history that seeks to retain normative force.

  POSITION: Key secondary source for understanding Habermas' break with
  first-generation Frankfurt School philosophy of history.
  },
  keywords = {Frankfurt-School, Adorno, Horkheimer, communicative-rationality, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{dallmayr1987discourse,
  author = {Dallmayr, Fred},
  title = {The Discourse of Modernity: Hegel and Habermas},
  journal = {Journal of Philosophy},
  year = {1987},
  volume = {84},
  number = {11},
  pages = {682--692},
  doi = {10.5840/JPHIL1987841118},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: Dallmayr examines the relationship between Hegel's philosophy
  of history and Habermas' theory of communicative action, asking whether
  Habermas' critique of the "philosophy of the subject" is fair to Hegel.
  He argues that Hegel's concept of ethical life (Sittlichkeit) anticipates
  some features of intersubjective reason that Habermas claims to have
  discovered only through the "paradigm shift" to communicative action.

  RELEVANCE: One of the earliest analyses of Habermas vs Hegel on philosophy
  of history; important for understanding whether Habermas' critique of Hegel
  is adequate or whether it misreads Hegel's intersubjective dimensions.

  POSITION: Critical secondary analysis; raises doubts about Habermas'
  characterization of Hegel as committed to a monological philosophy of
  the subject.
  },
  keywords = {Hegel, philosophy-of-subject, intersubjectivity, modernity, Medium, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

@article{habermas1973postscript,
  author = {Habermas, Jürgen and Lenhardt, Christian},
  title = {A Postscript to Knowledge and Human Interests},
  journal = {Philosophy of the Social Sciences},
  year = {1973},
  volume = {3},
  number = {2},
  pages = {157--189},
  doi = {10.1177/004839317300300204},
  note = {
  CORE ARGUMENT: In this postscript Habermas revises and clarifies the
  quasi-transcendental argument of Knowledge and Human Interests (1968),
  acknowledging weaknesses in his grounding of the knowledge-constitutive
  interests. He distances himself from the "transcendental" framing and moves
  toward a reconstructive methodology, beginning the transition that will
  culminate in the Theory of Communicative Action. The postscript also reaffirms
  the critique of Marx's conflation of labour and interaction.

  RELEVANCE: Key for tracking Habermas' self-revision and the development of
  his critique of historical materialism as a philosophy of history. The critique
  of Marx's labour paradigm here is the intellectual bridge to the mature theory
  of social evolution.

  POSITION: Primary source; documents transition from quasi-transcendental
  to reconstructive methodology in Habermas' anti-speculative stance.
  },
  keywords = {knowledge-human-interests, Marx, labour-paradigm, reconstruction, High, INCOMPLETE, no-abstract}
}

